    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2. Related work"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.08, 142.573, 510.666, 224.762 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "To our knowledge, the chain P was ﬁrst studied by Diaconis and Saloﬀ-Coste [6], who used a comparison with another chain on Ω studied by Hildebrand [8] to get an upper bound of O(n2) on the relaxation time (the inverse of the spectral gap), implying an upper bound of O(n4) on the ℓ2-mixing time. Then the results of Kassabov [9] on the Kazhdan constant yield an upper bound of O(n3) on the ℓ2-mixing time, which was the best known upper bound for total variation mixing as well. As for the lower bound, a simple counting"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 83.76, 110.653, 512.302, 140.755 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "argument shows that the total-variation mixing time can be lower bounded by Ω log n2n (which is actually an estimate of the diameter of the underlying graph, see Andre´n et al."
